":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $(X,Y)$ have joint density $\\tfrac{1}{18}$ on $0<x<3$, $0<y<6$, and zero elsewhere. For $A=\\{X<Y\\}$, you are given $\\mathbb{P}(A)=\\tfrac{3}{4}$. What is $f_{Y\\mid A}(y)$ for $0<y<6$, with zero elsewhere?","options":["$\\dfrac{\\min(y,3)}{18}$","$\\dfrac{2\\min(6-y,3)}{27}$","$\\dfrac{2\\min(y,3)}{27}$","$\\dfrac{1}{6}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ae788bec3b93795dad29620e","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For an event $A$ with $0<\\mathbb{P}(A)<1$, the Bayes form gives $f_{Y\\mid A}(y)\\le f_Y(y)/\\mathbb{P}(A)$. Suppose equality holds, with $f_Y(y)>0$, for every $y$ in an interval $I$. What does this say?","options":["$\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid Y=y)=1$ for $y\\in I$","$\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid Y=y)=\\mathbb{P}(A)$ for $y\\in I$","$\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid Y=y)=0$ for $y\\in I$","$\\mathbb{P}(A)=1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"df90e42f26b1479e1079d717","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}